rules.rule:
Generated preference and argument types

What: Don't hand-write types for `getPreferenceValues()` or command arguments. Raycast generates them in `raycast-env.d.ts` from `package.json`: use `Preferences` (extension-wide), `Preferences.<CommandName>` (per command) and `Arguments.<CommandName>`.

Why: Hand-written shapes drift from the manifest and hide renamed or removed preferences from the type checker.

Good:
```tsx
import { getPreferenceValues, LaunchProps } from "@raycast/api";

const { apiKey } = getPreferenceValues<Preferences.SearchIssues>();

export default function Command(props: LaunchProps<{ arguments: Arguments.SearchIssues }>) {
  const { query } = props.arguments;
}
```

Bad:
```tsx
interface Preferences {
  apiKey: string;
}
const prefs = getPreferenceValues<Preferences>();
const { apiKey } = getPreferenceValues<{ apiKey: string }>();
type CommandArguments = { query: string };
```

Don't flag: types for API responses, LocalStorage values, form values or tool inputs. Those aren't generated. `raycast-env.d.ts` itself must not be committed; see the no-generated-files rule.

Also correct: passing the generated type itself, e.g. `getPreferenceValues<Preferences>()` or `getPreferenceValues<Preferences.Index>()`. Never call that redundant; removing it makes the result `any`.

Also flag: `import { Preferences } from "@raycast/api"`, a deprecated export that shadows the generated type.

Pre-existing: if `main` already has a hand-written interface and this PR only adds a field to it, leave at most one note labelled pre-existing. Don't block on it.

Docs: https://developers.raycast.com/api-reference/preferences#getpreferencevalues

ESLint flat config

What: New extensions use the flat config from Raycast's docs, in `eslint.config.js` (CommonJS) or `eslint.config.mjs` (ESM):

```javascript
// eslint.config.js
const { defineConfig } = require("eslint/config");
const raycastConfig = require("@raycast/eslint-config");

module.exports = defineConfig([...raycastConfig]);
```

```javascript
// eslint.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from "eslint/config";
import raycastConfig from "@raycast/eslint-config";

export default defineConfig([...raycastConfig]);
```

Flag:
- a new extension that ships a legacy `.eslintrc*` file
- a PR that bumps `eslint` to 9 or later, or `@raycast/eslint-config` to 2 or later, but keeps `.eslintrc*`, since `ray lint` then breaks
- `defineConfig` imported from anywhere other than `eslint/config`

Don't flag: a config that exports `[...raycastConfig]` or `raycastConfig.flat()` without `defineConfig` (`@raycast/eslint-config` 2.x is already a flat array, so it lints fine), extra `ignores` entries, or older extensions on `main` that still use `.eslintrc.json` when the PR doesn't touch linting.

Docs: https://developers.raycast.com/information/developer-tools/eslint

Use trash() for user files

What: When an extension deletes the user's own files or folders, use `trash()` from `@raycast/api` (or `Action.Trash`) so the files can be recovered and the code works on macOS and Windows.

Good:
```typescript
import { trash } from "@raycast/api";
await trash(filePath);
```

Bad:
```typescript
execSync(`mv "${filePath}" ~/.Trash`);
execSync(`rm -rf "${filePath}"`);
```

Don't flag: `fs.rm` or `fs.unlink` on the extension's own temp or cache files (`environment.supportPath`, `os.tmpdir()`, its own downloads), where a recoverable delete isn't expected.

Wrap launchCommand in try/catch

What: `launchCommand` throws when the target command is disabled, not installed or doesn't exist. Wrap every call in `try/catch` and show the failure.

Good:
```ts
import { launchCommand, LaunchType } from "@raycast/api";
import { showFailureToast } from "@raycast/utils";

try {
  await launchCommand({ name: "my-command", type: LaunchType.UserInitiated });
} catch (error) {
  await showFailureToast(error, { title: "Could not open My Command" });
}
```

Bad:
```ts
await launchCommand({ name: "my-command", type: LaunchType.UserInitiated });
```

Don't flag calls already inside a `try` block or a `.catch()` chain.

Docs: https://developers.raycast.com/api-reference/command#launchcommand

No navigationTitle on the root view

What: Don't set `navigationTitle` on the view a command returns at its root (the default export's top-level `List`, `Grid`, `Detail` or `Form`). Raycast already shows the command title there. Put dynamic status in a section title, `subtitle`, accessories or detail metadata instead.

Good:
```tsx
export default function Command() {
  return <List searchBarPlaceholder="Search issues…">{/* … */}</List>;
}
// pushed view, which is fine
<Action.Push title="Show Details" target={<Detail navigationTitle={issue.key} markdown={md} />} />
```

Bad:
```tsx
export default function Command() {
  return <List navigationTitle="Search Issues">{/* … */}</List>;
}
```

Only flag when the view is clearly the command's root. Views shown with `Action.Push` or `push()` can set `navigationTitle`. If you can't tell whether a component is pushed or rendered at the root, don't flag it.

This includes shared components (an error view, or a form used both as the root and as a pushed view) when the command's default export returns them directly. In that case suggest passing `navigationTitle` only from the pushed call site.

Docs: https://developers.raycast.com/basics/prepare-an-extension-for-store#navigation-title

Several API reference examples set `navigationTitle` on a root view for illustration. If the author cites them, the Store guideline still applies. Also (nit): avoid long titles, and don't update `navigationTitle` repeatedly from state.

Failure toasts and error handling

What: In `catch` blocks that show an error to the user, prefer `showFailureToast(error, { title })` from `@raycast/utils` over hand-built `showToast({ style: Toast.Style.Failure, ... })`. `getSelectedText()`, `getSelectedFinderItems()`, `getFrontmostApplication()` and `WindowManagement.getActiveWindow()` reject when there's nothing to read, so wrap them in `try/catch`. `Clipboard.readText()` resolves to `undefined` when the clipboard is empty: ask for an `undefined` check, not a `try/catch`. Don't swallow errors from user-triggered actions without a toast or HUD.

Good:
```typescript
import { showFailureToast } from "@raycast/utils";
try {
  await saveItem(item);
} catch (error) {
  await showFailureToast(error, { title: "Could not save item" });
}
```

Bad:
```typescript
try { await saveItem(item); } catch (e) { await showToast({ style: Toast.Style.Failure, title: "Error", message: (e as Error).message }); }
try { await saveItem(item); } catch { /* ignored */ }
```

Don't flag: `useFetch`, `usePromise`, `useCachedPromise`, `useExec`, `useSQL`, `useAI` and `useStreamJSON` already show a failure toast with a Retry action by default, so don't ask for a second one. To change its text, suggest `failureToastOptions: { title, message, primaryAction }` (utils 1.16.0+) instead of an `onError` that only re-toasts. `showFailureToast` takes a `primaryAction`, so a single extra action is no reason for a manual toast. Leave alone manual toasts that need what `showFailureToast` can't do (a custom `message`, a `secondaryAction`, an Animated toast later switched to Failure), and existing code on `main` the PR doesn't touch.

If the extension pins `@raycast/utils` below 1.10.0 (no `showFailureToast`), a manual `showToast` is fine. Never call either toast during render; call it from an effect or event handler.

`showHUD` closes the main window. In a view command where the user should stay in Raycast, suggest `showToast` instead (nit).

Docs: https://developers.raycast.com/utilities/functions/showfailuretoast

Show errors in the view, not as an empty list

What: A `List` or `Grid` that loads remote or fallible data should render a failure state when loading fails, not the default "No results". Destructure `error` from `useFetch` / `usePromise` / `useCachedPromise` and render a `List.EmptyView` / `Grid.EmptyView` with a clear title, the error message and a retry or "Open Extension Preferences" action. A bad API key or network error should never look like "nothing found". The hook still shows its default failure toast unless `onError` is passed; that's fine alongside the empty view, so don't ask to remove it. If cached data exists, showing it with the toast is also fine.

Good:
```tsx
const { data, isLoading, error, revalidate } = useFetch<Item[]>(url);
return (
  <List isLoading={isLoading}>
    {error ? (
      <List.EmptyView
        icon={Icon.Warning}
        title="Could not load items"
        description={error.message}
        actions={<ActionPanel><Action title="Retry" onAction={revalidate} /></ActionPanel>}
      />
    ) : (
      data?.map((item) => <List.Item key={item.id} title={item.title} />)
    )}
  </List>
);
```

Bad:
```tsx
const { data, isLoading } = useFetch<Item[]>(url);
return <List isLoading={isLoading}>{data?.map((item) => <List.Item key={item.id} title={item.title} />)}</List>;
```

Severity: should fix on new extensions; a suggestion on updates that don't touch the loading code.

Docs: https://developers.raycast.com/basics/prepare-an-extension-for-store#empty-states and https://developers.raycast.com/information/best-practices#handle-errors

No shell injection

What: Never build a shell, AppleScript or PowerShell command by interpolating user input, file names, clipboard text, preference values or API data into a string. Run executables with an argument array (`execFile`/`spawn` from `node:child_process`, without `shell: true`), pass values into AppleScript through `runAppleScript(script, [value])` and `on run argv`, and into PowerShell as properly escaped literals or `execFile("powershell", [...])` (`runPowerShellScript` has no arguments parameter). `useExec` with `shell: true`, or a single interpolated command string, is the same problem; use `useExec(file, args)`. Don't disable sandboxing (e.g. Chromium `--no-sandbox`) to make something work.

Good:
```ts
import { execFile } from "node:child_process";
import { promisify } from "node:util";
await promisify(execFile)("git", ["-C", repoPath, "log", "--oneline", "-n", String(limit)]);
await runAppleScript(
  `on run argv
    tell application "Finder" to open POSIX file (item 1 of argv)
  end run`,
  [userPath],
);
```

Bad:
```ts
execSync(`git -C ${repoPath} log --oneline -n ${limit}`);
await runAppleScript(`tell application "Finder" to open POSIX file "${userPath}"`);
```

Don't flag fixed command strings with no interpolated values.

Destructive actions

What: An action that deletes, overwrites or sends something uses `style={Action.Style.Destructive}`. If it can't be undone, ask first with `confirmAlert` and `Alert.ActionStyle.Destructive`, using the `if (await confirmAlert(...))` form.

Good:
```tsx
<Action
  title="Delete Item"
  icon={Icon.Trash}
  style={Action.Style.Destructive}
  onAction={async () => {
    if (await confirmAlert({ title: "Delete Item?", primaryAction: { title: "Delete", style: Alert.ActionStyle.Destructive } })) {
      await deleteItem(item.id);
    }
  }}
/>
```

Bad:
```tsx
<Action title="Delete Item" onAction={() => deleteItem(item.id)} />
```

Don't flag recoverable deletes (`trash()`, archive, or anything with an undo), or actions inside a form whose submission is the confirmation.

Docs: https://developers.raycast.com/api-reference/user-interface/actions#action.style and https://developers.raycast.com/api-reference/feedback/alert#confirmalert
